      "question": {
        ":question/id": "3e4ef2ff-655d-5ddf-8444-3ae95311c64a",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/problem": "The three vectors share an origin, and each grid square has the same side length.\n\n![](images/2c/2c7df9724d07cffe1a36446d40ff4225a3e686e84fec3f4f5525ffe6e2f9060a.png)\n\nGiven $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}-4\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "First trace $-4\\mathbf{b}$ from the common origin $O$ to a point $P$: reverse its direction and scale its length. The remaining path from $P$ to the endpoint $C$ must be the unknown multiple of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\n![](images/30/3013d2520a7c1cfa9347e174a732a10d1e7707a39a731efe97e01e76d2f4dbc6.png)\n\nFollowing the grid direction of $\\mathbf{a}$ takes $2$ steps, while the parallel path $PC$ has $4$ steps. Their length ratio is $\\frac{4}{2}=2$. The remaining path points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{a}$, so\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PC}=-2\\mathbf{a}.\n$$\nAdding the path vectors gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{c}&=\\overrightarrow{OP}+\\overrightarrow{PC}\\\\\n&=-4\\mathbf{b}+(-2\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=-2\\mathbf{a}-4\\mathbf{b}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nBecause $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are nonparallel, these coefficients are unique. Comparing with $\\mathbf{c}=\\lambda\\mathbf{a}-4\\mathbf{b}$ gives $\\lambda=-2$. The minus sign records the reversed direction.",
